answer:
**Clarify first.** A structure that inserts at the end and removes in FIFO order is a queue. Ask: is the API still `push/pop/peek`? Which end does `pop` remove from? Is there a capacity limit? Is thread safety needed? State your assumption: `push` appends at the tail, `pop` removes from the head (FIFO).

**Design.** Write a generic class that hides the representation:

```java
class FifoStack<T> {
    private static class Node<T> { T val; Node<T> next; Node(T v){ val = v; } }
    private Node<T> head, tail;
    private int size;

    public void push(T x) {            // O(1): add at the end
        Node<T> n = new Node<>(x);
        if (tail == null) head = tail = n; else { tail.next = n; tail = n; }
        size++;
    }
    public T pop() {                   // O(1): remove from the front
        if (head == null) throw new NoSuchElementException();
        T v = head.val; head = head.next;
        if (head == null) tail = null;
        size--; return v;
    }
    public T peek() { if (head == null) throw new NoSuchElementException(); return head.val; }
    public boolean isEmpty() { return size == 0; }
    public int size() { return size; }
}
```

Both `push` and `pop` are O(1) because we keep both `head` and `tail`. Alternatives: `ArrayDeque` (circular array, usually faster in practice), or two stacks (amortised O(1)). In production you would just use `java.util.Queue`/`ArrayDeque`; implement it manually to show you understand the structure.

**Points to mention:** generics, private nested node class (encapsulation), custom exception vs returning null, `Iterable` if needed, and for concurrency a `ReentrantLock` or `LinkedBlockingQueue`.

**Test cases:** empty pop, single element (tail reset to null), many pushes then pops, ordering check.

Mistake to avoid: coding silently without noticing/naming that this is a queue.
